{
  "abstract": "The exact Coxeter-polynomial formula for the star trees T(p,q,r) and the convergence of their spectral radii as one arm grows. The three arms have p, q, r nodes, each counted WITH the centre, so E_m = T(2,3,m-3) and D_n = T(2,2,n-2). With [m] = 1 + X + ... + X^(m-1) and F_{p,q} = X^(p+q-2) - [p-1][q-1], five compared theorems: (1) for all p, q, r >= 2, with the generalized Cartan matrix given entrywise and the Coxeter element the ordered product of the reflections 1 - e_i e_i^T A of the geometric representation, (X - 1) * charpoly(c) = X^(r+1) F_{p,q} - F*_{p,q}, F* the reversal (Polynomial.reverse); (2) for (p,q) != (2,2), F_{p,q} has exactly one real root L(p,q) > 1, and L(p,q) < 2; (3) for (p,q) != (2,2) there are r0 >= 2 and lambda_r, the largest real eigenvalue of the Coxeter element of T(p,q,r) for r >= r0, with lambda_r in (1, L), every complex characteristic root z satisfying norm(z) <= lambda_r, strict increase in r, and convergence to L(p,q); thus these lambda_r are the Coxeter spectral radii; (4) for tau > 0, F_{p,q}(tau) = 0 if and only if (tau^-1 + ... + tau^-(p-1)) (tau^-1 + ... + tau^-(q-1)) = 1, the equation of the growth rate of the triangle Coxeter group (p,q,inf) (kernel-held in PALOMAR-2026-10-17-000013; only the equivalence of the two equations is proved here); (5) for rho the real root of x^3 = x + 1, rho <= L(p,q), with equality exactly at (p,q) = (2,3) and (3,2). These are classical results about Coxeter transformations and spectral limits of trees, relevant to Coxeter groups, spectral graph theory, and the study of Salem and Pisot limits. The all-parameter formula and one-arm spectral limit are main subjects of Evripidou's paper; this package treats its three-arm i = 0 family, extending the previously formalized E_m sequence to each fixed (p,q). Statements (2), (4) and (5) characterize and compare those limits. Through (4), (2) and (5) restate results of PALOMAR-2026-10-17-000013; that overlap is disclosed. No mathematical novelty or formalization priority is claimed. The compared statements use only core Mathlib vocabulary (Matrix.charpoly, Matrix.diagonal, List.finRange / List.prod, Finset.range and Finset.Icc sums, Polynomial.reverse, Polynomial.map, Polynomial.eval, Filter.Tendsto, nhds) — zero custom definitions on the compared surface.",
